      "page": {
        "page_number": 642,
        "text": "28.2.2 Bit Error Ratio\nA raw BER of 10-6 or better is required on each lane.\nA compliant receiver, when receiving from a compliant transmitter over a compliant\nchannel, shall deliver the specified raw BER to the subsequent FEC decoder. Error\nbursts with length more than 7 PAM4 symbols delivered to the PAM4 decoder shall\noccur with a probability of less than 1 in 1020 PAM4 symbols. To enable a more\npractical test, error bursts with length more than 3 PAM4 symbols delivered to the\nPAM4 decoder should occur with a probability of less than 1 in 1012 PAM4 symbols.\n28.2.3 Ground Differences\nThe ground difference between the driver and receiver shall be within the range\n±50 mV.\n28.2.4 Reference Models and Compliance Points\nCompliance points are defined at the die-channel interface. The reference model and\ncompliance points for the die-to-die channel are defined in 28.2.4.1. The compliance\npoints for the die transmitter and receiver are defined in 28.2.4.2.\n28.2.4.1 Reference Model and Compliance Points for the Die-to-Die Channel\nReference models and compliance points used to specify a CEI-112G-XSR+-PAM4\nimplementation of the die-to-die channel are provided in this section. A reference model\nfor the channel is provided in Figure 28-1. The channel includes a host ASIC package\nsubstrate, an assembly substrate or PCB, a module socket, and a module package\nsubstrate. The module socket is optional.\nThe channel is defined between test point T and test point R. The die bumps at T and R\nare not part of the channel.\nThe reference models provided for CEI-112G-XSR-PAM4 in Section 24.2.4.1 are also\nrelevant to CEI-112G-XSR+-PAM4.\nChannel characteristics are specified in Section 28.3.\n642 Optical Internetworking Forum - Clause 28: CEI-112G-XSR+-PAM4 Extended Extra Short Reach",
